WEBVTT

00:00.200 --> 00:02.947
An ultrasound film shows a heart in motion.

00:02.947 --> 00:07.356
Before birth, that motion can reveal how the chambers are developing.

00:10.200 --> 00:19.030
Our pilot study examined fetal echocardiograms from nine healthy pregnancies and thirteen with hypoplastic left heart syndrome.

00:20.200 --> 00:25.355
The published figures show how raw ultrasound frames were cleaned and cropped.

00:25.355 --> 00:28.923
Those images are research data, not invented examples.

00:30.200 --> 00:31.868
A heartbeat is a sequence.

00:31.868 --> 00:37.835
The study separated videos by cardiac cycle and prepared them as inputs for machine learning.

00:40.200 --> 00:43.888
Convolutional networks extracted features from the frames.

00:43.888 --> 00:48.465
Long short-term memory networks modeled information across the sequence.

00:50.200 --> 00:56.479
Five pretrained architectures were evaluated: MobileNet, two ResNets, DenseNet, and GoogLeNet.

00:56.479 --> 00:59.819
The strongest candidates informed a stacked model.

01:00.200 --> 01:03.099
The stacking framework combined three models.

01:03.099 --> 01:09.798
Here is the original architecture, alongside our explanatory animation of how predictions are assembled.

01:10.200 --> 01:19.532
These are genuine images from the article: healthy and hypoplastic left-heart ultrasound examples, displayed without invented abnormalities.

01:20.200 --> 01:27.379
Across five-fold cross-validation, the authors reported approximately ninety-point-five percent accuracy.

01:27.379 --> 01:29.361
The pilot's cohort was small.

01:30.200 --> 01:36.031
The comparison tables also report sensitivity, precision, specificity, and F-one score.

01:36.031 --> 01:39.650
A single accuracy number cannot replace those details.

01:40.200 --> 01:49.253
Patient selection, validation design, and missed cases matter enormously before an algorithm can be considered for prenatal clinical use.

01:50.200 --> 01:56.320
The promise is real, but limited: a research model found patterns in this pilot dataset.

01:56.320 --> 01:59.659
It was not validated as an autonomous diagnosis.
